---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/pipeline.json';

const n = (v: number, d = 0) =>
  v.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: d, maximumFractionDigits: d });

// Every status the feed carried in the window, and what each one claims. Kept here rather than in
// the export because it is vocabulary, not measurement.
const statuses = [
  ['Under way using engine', 'making way', 'typed'],
  ['Under way sailing', 'making way', 'typed'],
  ['Constrained by her draught', 'making way, with limited room', 'typed'],
  ['Restricted manoeuvrability', 'making way, limited ability to turn', 'typed'],
  ['At anchor', 'stationary', 'typed'],
  ['Moored', 'stationary', 'typed'],
  ['Aground', 'stationary, involuntarily', 'typed'],
  ['Not under command', 'unable to manoeuvre — says nothing about making way', 'typed'],
];
---
<Base
  title="How AIS works — what the ship types, and what the receiver measures"
  description="An AIS message mixes machine-measured position and speed with hand-entered status and vessel name. Knowing which is which is the whole trick."
>
  <h1>What's measured, and what's typed</h1>

  <p class="lede prose">
    An AIS broadcast looks like one coherent statement about a ship. It isn't. Half of it comes from
    a satellite receiver, and half of it comes from a person.
  </p>

  <h2>The two halves of a message</h2>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>field</th><th>where it comes from</th><th>can it be wrong?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Position (lat/lon)</td><td>GNSS receiver</td><td>Only if the equipment fails</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Speed over ground</td><td>GNSS receiver</td><td>Only if the equipment fails</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Course over ground</td><td>GNSS receiver</td><td>Only if the equipment fails</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Navigational status</strong></td><td><strong>Typed by the crew</strong></td><td><strong>Yes — silently</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Vessel name</strong></td><td><strong>Typed at installation</strong></td><td><strong>Yes — silently</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Call sign, IMO, dimensions</strong></td><td><strong>Typed at installation</strong></td><td><strong>Yes — silently</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">
    Navigational status is a dropdown on the transponder's keyboard-and-display unit. It is not
    derived from speed, not inferred from position, and nothing on board cross-checks it. Someone
    sets it, and it stays set until someone sets it again.
  </p>

  <p class="prose">
    That is what makes the comparison on this site possible at all. Both numbers travel in the
    <em>same message</em>, from the same box, at the same instant — so when they disagree, no
    question of clock skew, matching, or data fusion arises. The message contradicts itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The statuses a ship can claim</h2>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>status</th><th>what it claims</th><th>source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {statuses.map(([status, claim, source]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{status}</code></td><td>{claim}</td><td class="note">{source}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="prose note">
    <code>Not under command</code> is deliberately excluded from every finding on this site. It means
    a vessel cannot manoeuvre, which says nothing about whether it is moving — a broken-down ship
    still drifts, sometimes quickly. Counting it either way would invent a result.
  </p>

  <h2>How a contradiction is detected</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      <strong>On arrival.</strong> The pipeline finds stops from position alone: a run of fixes where
      the vessel stays put. It never consults the status to decide that. Only once a stop exists does
      it ask what the ship was claiming — and if the claim was "under way", it records the
      disagreement without resolving it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>On departure.</strong> A single message claiming <code>Moored</code> or
      <code>At anchor</code> while reporting speed above <strong>3 knots</strong>. That threshold
      isn't picked for effect: across all {n(227258)} messages claiming a stationary status, speed
      falls away smoothly to a minimum in the 2–3 knot band and then climbs again. Below the trough
      is a moored ship moving with the tide; above it is a ship under way.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why the numbers are never rounded up</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Neither reading is ever declared the winner. Both are stored, the conflict is flagged, and the
      count is published. Where a figure can't be supported — a stop whose true length is unknown
      because the observation window cut it short — it's shown as a bound rather than a number.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the irony at the centre of the whole thing: the ship <em>names</em> on this site come from
      the same hand-typed static data as the status being called out. Of {n(10703)} vessels seen,
      {' '}{n(9537)} broadcast a name and only {n(2783)} broadcast an IMO number — the one identifier
      that is actually assigned by a registry rather than typed into a box.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
